images.description:
*It demands the affective labour of adjacency* is a collective reading performance, part of Echos in den Hügeln festival at the Bibliothek Andreas Züst (14-15.11.2026). The sentence, borrowed from Tina Campt,  sets the vibe and suggests the topic of the Sunday reading session. For our purpose the “it” of the title refers to practices of hosting and reading, which both imply, in different ways, intimacy and proximity. Stemming from the research done during my residency at the Bibliothek in 2022 about comp(h)osting, fertile accumulations and residency aftercare, the reading will explore hospitality, intertextual intimacy and correspondence.&nbsp;

The reading will be broadcasted on Radio Robida and narrowcasted via FM in other rooms of Alpenhof. 

(Image: Rut Himmelsbach, Bibliothek Andreas Züst catalogue n.: KAEH 012)
